{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal claims it preserves committed-root validity because 'no committed root can be suspended.' This is a non sequitur under the stated problem: a committed root (a committed valid root) can still be suspended in the skeptical fixed-point semantics if its attackers are not themselves refuted. The RESIDUE annotation does nothing to establish committed-root validity; the target conflates 'root' with 'unattacked root' and thus fails to demonstrate that the validity of every committed root is preserved.","target":"09e5f5d65387d6ccaa8d3da749206c829f15b80bba30473318f60329f5127b05"},{"attack":true,"case":"Adding defense edges from a defender to a defended node is not semantics-preserving: it introduces a direct defense edge from defender to target, but under the ordinary grounded attack-only fixpoint run on the enriched graph, a direct edge is interpreted as an attack, not a defense. This inverts the intended reinstatement structure and can cause committed roots that were previously valid to be attacked and suspended, violating committed-root validity and determinism of the intended fixed point.","target":"94e1be29cd3e02ea572f7a97e892452d1098901311c289b79b63c7fba4657008"},{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal breaks reinstatement as a derived property. By defining acceptance as the intersection of all fixpoints respecting a total preorder, a node can be excluded from the intersection simply because, under some admissible preorder, one of its defenders is ranked low and a chain of attacks defeats it. In the standard grounded semantics that node would be reinstated once its attacker is refuted; here, the skeptical intersection over orders can leave it suspended across the entire family, so reinstatement no longer holds as a derived property of the intersection.","target":"be048cdc167ae82a97a2f05829d31f710143150b650d8e55b623ac7b0622b0bb"}]}